 ### [Deferred Filtering Architecture for Compressed Volumes](857-deferred-filtering-architecture-for-compressed-volumes.md)
-Deferred Filtering is a rendering architecture that performs high-quality shading of massive volumetric datasets by coordinating visibility- and resolution-aware adaptive data loading with GPU-accelerated per-frame data decompression, deblocking, and rendering. It solves the problem of blocking artifacts inherent in block-based compression methods (e.g., at low bit-rates) and the memory bandwidth limitations of decoding entire datasets. Unlike in-loop deblocking, which requires modification of the encoding method, this approach uses a post-loop strategy that supports multiple pluggable compression techniques without altering the compressed data format. It differs from prior single-resolution slice-based methods by generalizing deferred filtering to multi-resolution settings, combining levels of detail (LOD) with adaptive loading and visibility culling.
